persistcalls_sem_x86_64 -- the GH-1034 correctness fixture for option
`persistcalls` (tests/stages/kuna-persistcalls.xml).

What it is
  A 16,104-byte x86-64 ELF built from persistcalls_sem_x86_64.c. `revisit`
  (built at -O0) reads the two-byte u0.s[0] on both sides of a call and later
  rewrites u0 at four bytes through a pointer that only folds in a later
  heritage pass. `loopstore` holds a value across a loop of calls and stores it
  to a global after the loop. With every call left unguarded that the guards'
  placement allowed, the first printed `return 1;` and the second moved the
  store in front of the loop.

Why it is vendored
  The stage test pins the printed C, which depends on the exact code the
  compiler emits (CI's gcc 15 lays these functions out differently).

Where it came from
  gcc 11.4.0 (Ubuntu 11.4.0-1ubuntu1~22.04.3), GNU ld 2.38, x86-64 Linux:

      gcc -O1 -fno-pie -no-pie -fno-inline -fcf-protection=none -o persistcalls_sem_x86_64 persistcalls_sem_x86_64.c

  sha256 556200b1c027d65789a46add60d53fbbefd51466ae57bb8459e79f68dbc6f46b.
  Run with no arguments it prints 0 0 21 42.
